## API Response Conventions

All HTTP responses use the `ApiResponse` shape from `src/utils/responses.js`:

```json
{ "status": "success", "message": "...", "data": { ... } }
```

### HTTP Status Codes

| Code | When Used |
| --- | --- |
| `200 OK` | Successful read |
| `400 Bad Request` | Invalid query params |
| `404 Not Found` | `eventId` does not exist in the log |
| `409 Conflict` | Replay attempted on a non-failed notification |
| `500 Internal Server Error` | Unhandled exception |

---

## Custom Exceptions

Only the subset relevant to the HTTP management API:

| Class | Status | When to throw |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `BadRequest` | 400 | Invalid query params |
| `NotFound` | 404 | `eventId` not in notification log |
| `Conflict` | 409 | Replay on a non-failed notification |

**Rule:** whenever a new exception class is added, a matching `instanceof` block must be added to `exceptionHandler`.

---

## API Response Serializers

`src/utils/serializers/notification-serializer.js` transforms `NotificationLog` instances into API-safe objects. **Controllers only** — never call serializers from repositories or middleware.

```js
const notificationSerializer = require('../../utils/serializers/notification-serializer');

apiResponse.data = notificationSerializer.serializeNotification(log);
```

---

## Routing Conventions

- All API routes are versioned under `/api/v1/`.
- Route ordering matters: mount `/api/v1/notifications/stats` **before** `/api/v1/notifications/:eventId`.
- No authentication middleware — this is an internal service.

